data.content:
Yes! MSTest v3.3+ supports **tuple-based `DynamicData`**, giving you compile-time type safety by replacing `IEnumerable<object[]>` with `IEnumerable<(T1, T2)>`:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[DynamicData(nameof(TestData))]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}

public static IEnumerable<(string Input, bool Expected)> TestData =>
[
    ("valid@email.com", true),
    ("invalid", false),
    ("", false),
];
```

**What changed:**
- `IEnumerable<object[]>` → `IEnumerable<(string, bool)>` — type mismatches are now caught at compile time
- Square-bracket arrays → parenthesized tuples
- No casts needed inside the test method

For simple constant data like this, `[DataRow]` also works and is even simpler:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[DataRow("valid@email.com", true)]
[DataRow("invalid", false)]
[DataRow("", false)]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}
```

Use `DynamicData` with tuples when your test data is computed or involves non-constant types; use `[DataRow]` when all values are compile-time constants.
